Chapter 2

Mathematical Framework and Variable Definitions

2.1 Core State Variables

· E(t): usable civilizational power [W].
· P(t): population (number of autonomous agents).
· I(t): information‑processing capacity [bits s⁻¹].
· R(t): resilience (probability of surviving a major perturbation).
· A(t): inhabited spatial volume, a proxy for the total area of access to energy and matter flows [m² or number of settled systems].

2.2 The Survival Pressure Functional

The integrated hazard over a time horizon T_H is

\Lambda_{\text{int}}(t) = \int_t^{t+T_H} \Lambda(\tau)\,d\tau,

where \Lambda(\tau) = \lambda_{\text{exo}} + \lambda_{\text{endo}} + \lambda_0 T(\tau)^\kappa includes exogenous, endogenous, and technology‑scaled anthropogenic hazards (\kappa>0). The survival pressure is

S(\Lambda) = 1 - \exp(-\Lambda_{\text{int}}),

so that S\to 1 when extinction is nearly certain on the timescale T_H, and S\to 0 when it is negligible.

