Chapter 3

Derivation of the Expansion Equation and Saddle‑Node Bifurcation

3.1 Microscopic Settlement Model

We consider a civilization that can establish settlements, each of average area a_0, up to a planetary maximum n_{\max}=A_{\max}/a_0. Let n(t) be the number of occupied units, so that A(t)=a_0 n(t). The Allee threshold is n_c = A_c/a_0. Each settlement can replicate (colonise a new site) with per‑capita rate

\alpha(A) = r\left(\frac{A}{A_c}-1\right)\left(1-\frac{A}{A_{\max}}\right) \quad\text{for } A\ge A_c,

and zero otherwise. Here r = \beta T S(\Lambda) encodes the survival pressure S and the technological conductivity T. Settlements go extinct at a total rate \mu = \mu_0 + \gamma E^\delta, where \mu_0 is the natural failure rate and \gamma E^\delta the energy‑cost penalty.

The master equation for the probability P_n(t) is

\frac{dP_n}{dt} = B(n-1)P_{n-1} + D(n+1)P_{n+1} - [B(n)+D(n)]P_n,

with B(n)=n\,\alpha(a_0 n) and D(n)=\mu n.

3.2 Mean‑Field Limit via System‑Size Expansion

Introduce the system size \Omega = n_{\max} and write n = \Omega\phi + \Omega^{1/2}\xi. Expanding the master equation in powers of \Omega^{-1/2} (van Kampen, 1992) gives at leading order the macroscopic rate equation

\frac{d\phi}{dt} = b(\phi) - d(\phi),

where b(\phi) = \phi\,r(\phi/\phi_c-1)(1-\phi) and d(\phi)=\mu\phi. Multiplying by A_{\max} and using A = A_{\max}\phi yields the deterministic expansion equation

\boxed{\frac{dA}{dt} = r A\left(\frac{A}{A_c}-1\right)\left(1-\frac{A}{A_{\max}}\right) - \mu A}. \tag{3.1}

The first term encodes the growth with Allee saturation; the second is the linear loss.

3.3 Equilibrium Analysis

Equation (3.1) always admits the trivial equilibrium A=0. Non‑zero equilibria satisfy

r\left(\frac{A}{A_c}-1\right)\left(1-\frac{A}{A_{\max}}\right) = \mu. \tag{3.2}

Define the dimensionless fraction x = A/A_{\max} and the ratio c = A_c/A_{\max} \in (0,1). Then (3.2) becomes

r\left(\frac{x}{c}-1\right)(1-x) = \mu.

The left‑hand side is a concave quadratic with a maximum at x_m = (1+c)/2 of value r(1-c)^2/(4c). Hence a positive equilibrium exists if and only if

r \ge r_c \equiv \mu\frac{4c}{(1-c)^2}. \tag{3.3}

At r = r_c the two positive roots coalesce at x_0 = (1+c)/2.

3.4 Saddle‑Node Bifurcation

Set u = x - x_0 and \varepsilon = r - r_c. Taylor expansion of the right‑hand side G(x,r) around (x_0,r_c) gives

G(x_0+u,\, r_c+\varepsilon) = \alpha\varepsilon - \beta u^2 + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon u, u^3),

with

\alpha = \left.\frac{\partial G}{\partial r}\right|_{x_0,r_c} = \frac{(1+c)(1-c)^2}{8c} > 0,

\beta = -\frac{1}{2}\left.\frac{\partial^2 G}{\partial x^2}\right|_{x_0,r_c} = \frac{r_c}{2}\left(1+\frac{1}{c}\right) > 0.

Thus, near the bifurcation,

\frac{du}{dt} \approx \alpha\varepsilon - \beta u^2.

After a rescaling v = \sqrt{\beta/\alpha}\,u and \tau = \sqrt{\alpha\beta}\,t, this becomes the normal form

\frac{dv}{d\tau} = \tilde{\varepsilon} - v^2,

with \tilde{\varepsilon} = \varepsilon. For \tilde{\varepsilon} < 0 there are no positive fixed points; for \tilde{\varepsilon} > 0 two fixed points appear: a stable node at v = +\sqrt{\tilde{\varepsilon}} and an unstable node at v = -\sqrt{\tilde{\varepsilon}}. In the original variables, the stable equilibrium A^* > A_{\max}(1+c)/2 exists for r > r_c and is an attractor; the smaller root is a repelling threshold. This is a canonical saddle‑node bifurcation, implying a first‑order phase transition in civilizational spatial extent as the survival pressure crosses the critical value S_c defined by r_c = \beta T S_c.

