Chapter 4

Information Thermodynamics and the Virtualisation Decision Boundary

4.1 Landauer’s Principle and Computational Budget

Landauer’s principle sets the minimum energy to erase one bit: E_{\text{bit}} = k_B T \ln 2 \approx 2.9\times 10^{-21} J at 300 K. For a civilization allocating a fraction \epsilon of its power E to computation, the information processing rate I obeys

\frac{dI}{dt} = \eta\,\epsilon E - \delta_I I, \tag{4.1}

where \eta \le 1/E_{\text{bit}} is the bit‑erasure efficiency (bits per joule).

4.2 Margolus–Levitin Bound

The maximum number of logical operations per second that can be performed with an energy \Delta E is bounded by 2\Delta E/\pi\hbar. Hence, for a computational power E_{\text{comp}} = \epsilon E, the ultimate processing rate is

\dot{\mathcal{O}}_{\max} = \frac{2\epsilon E}{\pi\hbar}.

This places a hard ceiling on virtualisation at any fixed energy budget: to keep expanding its inner space, a civilization must continually increase E.

4.3 Virtualisation vs. Expansion Decision Boundary

Let the survival benefit (utility) be a smooth function U(I,A). The marginal survival gain per joule allocated to computation is (\partial U/\partial I)\,\eta. The marginal gain per joule allocated to physical expansion is (\partial U/\partial A)\,(\gamma E^\delta)^{-1} (since maintaining an additional unit of area costs \gamma E^\delta watts). The boundary between the virtualised and expansionist attractors is given by equality of these marginal returns:

\frac{\partial U}{\partial I}\,\eta = \frac{\partial U}{\partial A}\,\frac{1}{\gamma E^\delta}. \tag{4.2}

When the left‑hand side exceeds the right, virtualisation is favoured; otherwise, expansion dominates. With explicit utility forms (e.g., U(I,A) = 1 - e^{-aI - bA}), equation (4.2) provides a quantitative decision boundary.

