Chapter 6

Multiplanetarity and Correlated Failure

6.1 Independent vs. Correlated Worlds

If N settlements have independent extinction probabilities p, total extinction probability is p^N. In reality, settlements are correlated (shared star, communication‑borne threats, biological monoculture). We model this via a one‑factor Gaussian copula. Let each settlement have a latent variable

Z_i = \sqrt{\rho}\,\mathcal{E} + \sqrt{1-\rho}\,\varepsilon_i,

with \mathcal{E},\varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) independent. A settlement fails if Z_i < \tau, where \tau = \Phi^{-1}(p) ensures the marginal failure probability is p. The probability that all N settlements fail is

P(\text{all fail}) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \phi(\mathcal{E})\,\left[\Phi\!\left(\frac{\tau - \sqrt{\rho}\,\mathcal{E}}{\sqrt{1-\rho}}\right)\right]^N d\mathcal{E}.

For \rho=0 this reduces to \Phi(\tau)^N = p^N, as required. For \rho>0 and large N, the dependence on the common factor \mathcal{E} strongly increases the joint failure probability relative to the independent case. The survival advantage of multiplanetarity is maximised by spreading settlements across independent stellar systems, where \rho is negligible.

6.2 The Expansion Propensity Filter

Not all civilizations that cross the thermodynamic threshold will actually expand. Social, political, and institutional factors are modelled by a propensity parameter \theta \in [0,1], the probability that a civilization at the threshold initiates sustained expansion. The effective fraction of expanding civilizations is p_{\text{exp}} = \theta\,\mathbb{P}(r > r_c).

