Chapter 8

Empirical Calibration

8.1 Polynesian Colonisation (AD 800–1300)

We fit the expansion equation (3.1) to the cumulative number of permanently occupied major islands in East Polynesia (Wilmshurst et al., 2011). Using nonlinear least squares (Levenberg–Marquardt) with six data points and three parameters (r, c = A_c/A_{\max}, \mu), we obtain

r = 0.019\ \text{yr}^{-1}, \qquad c = 0.082, \qquad \mu = 9.2\times 10^{-5}\ \text{yr}^{-1}.

Bootstrap 95 % confidence intervals (based on resampling residuals, acknowledging the small sample) are r \in [0.015,0.024], c \in [0.065,0.103], \mu \in [7.1, 11.6]\times 10^{-5}. The inferred critical survival pressure S_c is approximately 0.65. The model captures the slow initial phase, the explosive pulse around AD 1100–1200, and the subsequent saturation. We note that the archaeological chronology is debated; the Wilmshurst et al. short chronology may be systematically late, and the true ages could be older by up to 200 years. The fitted parameters are robust within the uncertainty of the dates, but the confidence intervals should be regarded as lower bounds.

8.2 Out‑of‑Sample Test: Age of Exploration

We apply the identical logistic‑threshold form to the European overseas colonial expansion (1415–1800), using major colonies as a proxy for settled area. The only free parameter is a technology scaling factor T_{\text{Europe}}/T_{\text{Polynesia}}, estimated independently from ship speeds and tonnage to be 3.0 \pm 0.8. Using the median value 3.0, the model predicts that 50 % of the eventual saturation is reached by AD 1580; the historical midpoint falls at AD 1590. The root‑mean‑square error over the full curve is 0.82 islands. The agreement persists for scaling factors in the range 2–4, demonstrating robustness. This supports the universality of the logistic‑threshold dynamic across human expansion epochs.

8.3 Extrapolation to Space

The model, with parameters calibrated from historical expansions, predicts that a sustained human push to interplanetary scale requires crossing an Allee threshold equivalent to a permanent lunar settlement. The timescale from threshold crossing to 90 % saturation of the inner Solar System is estimated at 80–120 years. This prediction is conditional on the assumption that survival pressure and institutional filters remain in the super‑critical regime; it is not a deterministic forecast.

