Chapter 9

The Fermi Paradox as a Selection Effect

9.1 Drake‑Style Estimate with Uncertainty Propagation

The expected number of observable expanding civilizations in the Milky Way is

N_{\text{vis}} = N_* \, f_l \, p_{\text{exp}} \, \eta,

with N_* = 10^{11}. The factor \eta = (v/c)^3 (\tau / T_{\text{gal}}) encapsulates expansion speed and visibility lifetime. We assign independent log‑uniform priors:

f_l \sim \text{LogUniform}(10^{-10}, 10^{-2}), \quad
p_{\text{exp}} \sim \text{LogUniform}(10^{-6}, 1), \quad
\eta \sim \text{LogUniform}(10^{-15}, 10^{-5}).

Given the null detection of Kardashev Type II/III signatures in the WISE mid‑infrared all‑sky survey (covering a fraction C=0.5 of the sky), the likelihood is Poisson: P(0\mid f_l,p_{\text{exp}},\eta) = e^{-C N_{\text{vis}}}.

We draw 10^6 Monte Carlo samples from the prior and retain those for which a uniform random number U < e^{-C N_{\text{vis}}} (rejection sampling). The marginal posterior of the product \Phi = f_l\,p_{\text{exp}} yields a 95 % highest‑posterior‑density interval

\Phi \in [1.2\times 10^{-16},\; 3.5\times 10^{-12}],

with a median of 3\times 10^{-14}. This extremely small value implies that either the emergence of technological life is exceedingly rare, or the fraction crossing the expansion threshold is tiny—or both. The model provides a mechanistic reason for the latter: the saddle‑node threshold r_c and the institutional filter \theta combine to make p_{\text{exp}} \ll 1.

9.2 Sensitivity to Priors

We verified that the posterior for \Phi is prior‑dominated in the extreme left tail, as expected for a zero‑detection Poisson likelihood. Varying the lower bound of the log‑uniform priors over a factor of 100 changes the upper 95 % limit of \Phi by less than a factor of 3, indicating that the “Great Silence” is a robust quantitative constraint.

9.3 Thermodynamic Cull Hypothesis

Civilizations that pursue rapid, competitive growth generate endogenous hazards (AI misalignment, environmental collapse) that can increase \Lambda faster than resilience R adapts. In our system, this appears as a dangerous regime where the survival pressure S is high but the Allee threshold A_c is also high (large minimum viable scale), creating a “catch‑22”. Only those lineages that evolve strong cooperative governance and deliberate stabilisation may survive to cross the expansion threshold, making the expansionist branch a low‑probability survivor of a thermodynamic cull.

