Chapter 11

Conclusion

We have demonstrated, through rigorous derivation and empirical calibration, that the spatial expansion of civilizations can be understood as a threshold‑dependent survival strategy rooted in non‑equilibrium thermodynamics. The first‑order phase transition in the settlement equation explains why expansion is not gradual but explosive, and why most civilizations in the universe may remain confined, virtualise, or collapse. The Fermi paradox is thus a natural consequence of the model’s parameter space. The road to the stars, if we travel it, will be a thermodynamic phase transition enacted by a species that navigated the filters and chose survival on the largest scale.

Appendix A — Master Equation and Kramers–Moyal Expansion for Settlement Dynamics

A.1  Microscopic model

Consider a civilisation that can establish up to n_{\max} settlement units on its home planet, each of average area a_0. The total inhabited area is A = a_0 n.
A settlement can reproduce (found a new unit) with per‑capita rate

\alpha(A) = r\Bigl(\frac{A}{A_c}-1\Bigr)\Bigl(1-\frac{A}{A_{\max}}\Bigr) \qquad (A\ge A_c),

and \alpha(A)=0 otherwise. The parameter r = \beta T S(\Lambda) carries the survival pressure and the technological flow conductivity; A_c is the Allee threshold and A_{\max} the planetary carrying capacity.
Each unit dies at a total rate \mu = \mu_0 + \gamma E^{\delta}, where \mu_0 is the natural failure rate and the second term represents the energetic cost of maintenance.

A.2  Master equation

Let P_n(t) be the probability that exactly n settlements exist at time t. The transition rates are

\begin{aligned}
B(n) &= n\,\alpha(a_0 n) \quad &\text{(birth)}\\
D(n) &= \mu n \quad &\text{(death)} .
\end{aligned}

The master equation reads

\frac{dP_n}{dt} = B(n-1)P_{n-1} + D(n+1)P_{n+1} - \bigl[B(n)+D(n)\bigr]P_n . \tag{A.1}

A.3  System‑size expansion (van Kampen)

Write the system size \Omega = n_{\max} and expand

n = \Omega\phi + \Omega^{1/2}\xi ,

where \phi(t) is the deterministic fraction and \xi describes fluctuations of order \Omega^{1/2}.
The birth and death rates are smooth functions of the intensive variable \phi:

\begin{aligned}
B(\Omega\phi) &= \Omega\, \phi\, r\Bigl(\frac{\phi}{\phi_c}-1\Bigr)(1-\phi) \equiv \Omega\,b(\phi) ,\\
D(\Omega\phi) &= \mu\,\Omega\phi \equiv \Omega\,d(\phi) .
\end{aligned}

Insert the ansatz into the master equation and expand the probability distribution in powers of \Omega^{-1/2}.
The leading order (O(\Omega^0)) yields the macroscopic rate equation

\frac{d\phi}{dt} = b(\phi) - d(\phi). \tag{A.2}

Multiplying by A_{\max} = \Omega a_0 and using A = A_{\max}\phi gives the deterministic expansion equation

\boxed{\frac{dA}{dt} = rA\Bigl(\frac{A}{A_c}-1\Bigr)\Bigl(1-\frac{A}{A_{\max}}\Bigr) - \mu A}. \tag{A.3}

The next order yields a linear Fokker–Planck equation for the fluctuations \xi,

\frac{\partial\Pi(\xi,t)}{\partial t} = -\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi}\bigl[\mathcal{J}'(\phi)\,\xi\,\Pi\bigr] + \frac{1}{2}\bigl[b(\phi)+d(\phi)\bigr]\frac{\partial^{2}\Pi}{\partial\xi^{2}} ,

where \mathcal{J}(\phi)=b(\phi)-d(\phi). For large \Omega the fluctuations are small, justifying the mean‑field ODE (A.3) for macroscopic evolution.

