Appendix B — Centre‑Manifold Reduction and Normal Form of the Saddle‑Node Bifurcation

B.1  Dimensionless form and critical point

Introduce x = A/A_{\max} and c = A_c/A_{\max} (0<c<1). Equation (A.3) becomes

\frac{dx}{dt} = G(x,r) \equiv r\,x\Bigl(\frac{x}{c}-1\Bigr)(1-x) - \mu x . \tag{B.1}

Stationary states x^{*}>0 satisfy

r\Bigl(\frac{x}{c}-1\Bigr)(1-x) = \mu .

The left‑hand side is a concave quadratic vanishing at x=c and x=1, with a maximum at

x_m = \frac{1+c}{2}, \qquad H_{\max} = r\frac{(1-c)^{2}}{4c}.

Non‑zero equilibria exist iff H_{\max}\ge \mu, i.e.

r \ge r_c \equiv \mu\frac{4c}{(1-c)^{2}}. \tag{B.2}

At r=r_c the two positive roots merge at x_0 = (1+c)/2.

B.2  Taylor expansion

Set

\varepsilon = r - r_c, \qquad u = x - x_0 .

Expand G(x,r) around (x_0,r_c):

G(x_0+u,r_c+\varepsilon) = G(x_0,r_c) + \frac{\partial G}{\partial r}\Big|_0 \varepsilon + \frac{\partial G}{\partial x}\Big|_0 u + \frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial^{2} G}{\partial x^{2}}\Big|_0 u^{2} + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon u, u^{3}).

Because x_0 is a double root, G(x_0,r_c)=0 and \partial G/\partial x|_0 = 0. The non‑zero coefficients are

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial G}{\partial r}\Big|_0 &= x_0\Bigl(\frac{x_0}{c}-1\Bigr)(1-x_0) 
= \frac{(1+c)(1-c)^{2}}{8c} > 0,\\[4pt]
\frac{\partial^{2} G}{\partial x^{2}}\Big|_0 &= -2r_c\Bigl(\frac{1}{c}+1\Bigr) .
\end{aligned}

Therefore

\frac{du}{dt} \approx \alpha\varepsilon - \beta u^{2},

with

\alpha = \frac{(1+c)(1-c)^{2}}{8c}, \qquad
\beta = -\frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial^{2} G}{\partial x^{2}}\Big|_0 = r_c\Bigl(\frac{1}{c}+1\Bigr) > 0 .

B.3  Normal form

Rescale v = \sqrt{\beta/\alpha}\,u and \tau = \sqrt{\alpha\beta}\,t. Then

\frac{dv}{d\tau} = \varepsilon - v^{2} \equiv \tilde\varepsilon - v^{2}. \tag{B.3}

For \tilde\varepsilon<0 there are no positive fixed points; for \tilde\varepsilon>0 a stable node (v = +\sqrt{\tilde\varepsilon}) and an unstable node (v = -\sqrt{\tilde\varepsilon}) appear. This is the canonical saddle‑node bifurcation. In terms of the original variables, a stable expanded state A^{*} > A_{\max}x_0 exists iff r > r_c, and the transition at r=r_c is discontinuous—a first‑order phase transition in the civilisation’s spatial extent.

A centre‑manifold reduction (not shown) confirms that all fast degrees of freedom are slaved to v near the bifurcation, so (B.3) captures the full dynamics up to smooth coordinate changes.

