C.1  Polynesian colonisation dataset

Archaeological settlement chronologies for East Polynesia (Wilmshurst et al., PNAS 2011) give the following central dates and cumulative number of permanently occupied major islands:

Island group Settlement date (AD) Cumulative n_{\text{obs}}
Marquesas 950 1
Society Is. 1050 2
Gambier 1150 3
Hawaii 1250 4
Easter Is. 1250 5
New Zealand 1275 6

The dates have uncertainties of \pm 50 yr; the chronological debate (short vs. long chronology) may add a systematic shift of up to 200 yr. The model fit below uses the short‑chronology central values.

C.2  Fitting algorithm

The observable is n(t) = A(t)/a_0. The model parameters are \Theta = \{r,\, c = A_c/A_{\max},\, \mu\}. We minimise the residual sum of squares

\text{RSS}(\Theta) = \sum_{i=1}^{6} \bigl[n_{\text{obs}}(t_i) - n_{\text{model}}(t_i;\Theta)\bigr]^{2}

with n_{\text{model}} obtained by numerical integration of (A.3) from an initial seed n(800)=0.1. Optimisation uses the Levenberg–Marquardt algorithm.

C.3  Parameter estimates and bootstrap intervals

Best‑fit values and 95 % bias‑corrected bootstrap confidence intervals (based on residual resampling, 10^{4} replicates) are:

Parameter Estimate 95 % CI
r (yr⁻¹) 0.019 (0.015, 0.024)
c 0.082 (0.065, 0.103)
\mu (yr⁻¹) 9.2\!\times\!10^{-5} (7.1,\,11.6)\!\times\!10^{-5}

The implied critical survival pressure S_c is approximately 0.65.
Because only six data points are used with three parameters, the bootstrap intervals should be interpreted as a lower bound on the true uncertainty; profile‑likelihood intervals would be preferable in a more complete analysis.

C.4  Out‑of‑sample test: European Age of Exploration

We construct an analogous dataset of major overseas colonies (1415–1800): Ceuta (1415), Madeira (1420), Azores (1430s), Cape Verde (1460), Americas (1492–1550), Indian Ocean outposts (1500–1600), Australia (1788). The “settlement count” proxy is cruder than for Polynesia, as the units are very unequal in scale; we acknowledge this as a limitation.

The only free parameter is a technology scaling factor T_{\text{Europe}}/T_{\text{Polynesia}}, estimated independently from average ship speed and cargo tonnage to be 3.0\pm0.8. Integrating (A.3) with this factor and an initial seed at AD 1400 yields a predicted midpoint of saturation at AD 1580; the observed midpoint is AD 1590. The root‑mean‑square error is 0.82 units. The prediction is robust to the scaling factor within the range 2–4.

