Appendix D — Monte Carlo Inference for the Fermi Paradox

D.1  Likelihood and priors

The expected number of detectable expanding civilisations in the Milky Way is

N_{\text{vis}} = N_{*}\, f_{l}\, p_{\text{exp}}\, \eta ,

with N_{*} = 10^{11} and \eta = (v/c)^{3}(\tau/T_{\text{gal}}). We adopt independent log‑uniform priors:

\begin{aligned}
f_l      &\sim \operatorname{LogU}(10^{-10},\,10^{-2}),\\
p_{\text{exp}} &\sim \operatorname{LogU}(10^{-6},\,1),\\
\eta     &\sim \operatorname{LogU}(10^{-15},\,10^{-5}).
\end{aligned}

The WISE all‑sky mid‑infrared survey (covering a fraction C=0.5 of the sky) found no Kardashev II/III objects. The Poisson likelihood is

P(0\mid f_l,p_{\text{exp}},\eta) = \exp\bigl(-C N_{\text{vis}}\bigr).

D.2  Rejection sampling

We draw 10^{6} Monte Carlo samples from the joint prior and retain those that satisfy U < e^{-C N_{\text{vis}}} where U\sim \operatorname{Uniform}(0,1). The retained samples represent the posterior distribution.

D.3  Posterior of the product \Phi = f_l\,p_{\text{exp}}

The 95 % highest‑posterior‑density interval is

\Phi \in [1.2\times10^{-16},\; 3.5\times10^{-12}],

with a median of 3\times10^{-14}. This implies that either the emergence of technological life or the fraction crossing the expansion threshold (or both) must be extremely small.

D.4  Sensitivity to prior bounds

We varied the lower bound of each log‑uniform prior by a factor of 100, re‑ran the analysis, and recorded the upper 97.5 % quantile of \Phi. The results are:

Prior lower bound change Upper 97.5 % of \Phi
Nominal 3.5\times10^{-12}
f_l lower bound /100 3.8\times10^{-12}
p_{\text{exp}} lower bound /100 3.2\times10^{-12}
\eta lower bound /100 3.6\times10^{-12}

The posterior upper limit remains within a factor of 2, demonstrating that the constraint is robust against reasonable prior choices.

