Appendix E — Correlated Failure and the Gaussian Copula (Complete Derivation)

E.1  Latent variable model

Consider N settlements with equal marginal failure probability p. Introduce latent variables

Z_i = \sqrt{\rho}\,\mathcal{E} + \sqrt{1-\rho}\,\varepsilon_i, \qquad \mathcal{E},\varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) \text{ independent}.

Define failure by Z_i < \tau with \tau = \Phi^{-1}(p). Then P(Z_i<\tau) = \Phi(\tau) = p as required.

E.2  Joint failure probability

The probability that all N settlements fail is

P(\text{all fail}) = \mathbb{P}\!\left(\bigcap_{i=1}^{N}\{Z_i < \tau\}\right)
= \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \phi(\mathcal{E}) \left[ \Phi\!\left(\frac{\tau - \sqrt{\rho}\,\mathcal{E}}{\sqrt{1-\rho}}\right) \right]^{N} d\mathcal{E}. \tag{E.1}

For \rho=0 the expression reduces to \Phi(\tau)^{N} = p^{N}, the independent case. For \rho>0, the integral captures the increased joint failure probability due to the common factor \mathcal{E}.

E.3  Large‑N approximation

When N is large and p small, the inner term \Phi(\cdot) can be approximated by its tail. Expanding for large negative \mathcal{E} yields

P(\text{all fail}) \approx \left[ \Phi\!\left( \sqrt{\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}}\,\tau \right) \right]^{N},

which is exponentially larger than p^{N}. This result mirrors the Vasicek large‑portfolio approximation in credit risk.

E.4  Implications for multiplanetarity

The survival advantage of spreading settlements across different stellar systems comes from driving \rho to nearly zero, thereby recovering the p^{N} scaling. Interstellar separation thus provides the strongest reduction of correlated existential threats.

